<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- suez (MT8173/MT6630): this device's WiFi/BT combo chip kernel
         driver predates WPA3 and has no SAE support at all (see the
         suez-wpa3-sae-gap memory): confirmed the same firmware binary as
         the stock Amazon ROM, so the chip's firmware itself likely does
         support SAE, but the host kernel driver never learned to ask for
         it (rejects the SAE AKM suite outright).

         With config_wifiSaeUpgradeEnabled left at its AOSP default (true),
         ClientModeImpl.updateWifiConfigOnStartConnection() rewrites a
         saved WPA2-PSK network to SECURITY_TYPE_SAE whenever an in-range
         AP advertises WPA3-Transition mode (both PSK and SAE in its RSN
         IE): this is intentional upstream behavior for capable devices,
         but on this device it hands wpa_supplicant an SAE-only network
         block. wpa_supplicant's own driver-capability-based AKM
         stripping (WPA_DRIVER_FLAGS_SAE, wpa_supplicant.c) would
         correctly fall back to PSK if the network block still listed
         both, but by then SAE is the *only* option left, so the
         fallback has nothing to fall back to and the connection fails
         ("WPA: Failed to select authenticated key management type").

         Disabling the auto-upgrade keeps saved PSK networks as PSK even
         against a transition-mode AP, letting the connection proceed
         with WPA2 end-to-end, which this driver already handles fine.
         This does not help true WPA3-only (SAE-required, no PSK) networks;
         that gap remains open pending real SAE support in the kernel
         driver.
    -->
    <bool translatable="false" name="config_wifiSaeUpgradeEnabled">false</bool>
</resources>
